    "message": "fix(analytics): record reasoning and tool calls, not just prose\n\nOpening a request in the dashboard showed \"(not captured)\" for the output\nof most turns. The read path was fine: the detail route selects the row\nwhole and the UI renders output_text as given. Nothing was ever written.\n\n_parse_frame handled exactly one delta type:\n\n    if isinstance(delta, dict) and delta.get(\"type\") == \"text_delta\":\n\nAn assistant turn streams three. thinking_delta and input_json_delta were\ndropped on the floor, so a turn that only called tools -- the ordinary\nshape under Claude Code -- stored an empty string. Measured on a live\n17,825-row log, over the last 24h:\n\n    (has_tools, has_output) -> n\n    (False, True)      5      no tools: captured every time\n    (True,  False)  4728      tools, no prose: nothing stored\n    (True,  True)    453      tools plus prose: the prose was stored\n\nNot one zero-output row lacked tools, and those rows are not empty\nresponses: tokens_out averages 148 and peaks at 6,725. The frames were\nparsed correctly all along -- tokens_out comes from the same parser --\nonly the delta types were filtered out.\n\nThis is not a recent regression. git log -L over _parse_frame shows the\ntext_delta-only condition has been there since the original analytics\ncommit; what changed is that traffic shifted to tool-heavy sessions,\ntaking the captured share from 55% in July to under 10%.\n\nCapture now tracks content_block_start to learn each block's type and\ntool name, then routes deltas to prose, reasoning, or per-index tool\nargument buffers. Reasoning and tool arguments are request bodies, so\nthey follow REQUEST_LOG_CAPTURE_BODIES; the character and call counts are\nkept either way so list views can show the shape of a turn without\nloading its transcript. A stream cut mid-arguments keeps the fragment\nrather than discarding the call.\n\nThe detail view renders the three kinds as three kinds, instead of one\nundifferentiated <pre>: reasoning in a collapsed violet pane, tool calls\nas name chips with pretty-printed arguments, prose on its own. Empty is\nno longer one condition -- \"this turn called tools without writing a\nreply\" and \"bodies are not being stored\" now say so. The table gains a\nTurn column so the shape is visible without opening each row.\n\nAlso on the analytics tab: the charts were drawn in hardcoded blue and\nred against an emerald UI and stretched to a fixed 640x180 bitmap, so\nthey were both off-palette and soft on any HiDPI screen. They now read\ndesign tokens, scale to devicePixelRatio, and carry a value scale.\n\nVerification:\n- 10 new tests covering tool-only turns, reasoning kept out of the reply,\n  frames split across arbitrary chunk boundaries, out-of-order blocks,\n  truncated arguments, bodies-off, and the complete-message path\n- schema migration exercised against a populated legacy-schema database\n  and against the real on-disk log\n- 5/5 local CI; 3100 passed, same 14 failures as the unmodified base\n  (8 need real API keys, 6 are the PowerShell installer tests)\n- rendered against the real assets in a browser: desktop, 390px mobile,\n  keyboard focus reaches the reasoning pane through the modal focus trap\n\nNot done: historical rows keep their empty output; only new requests\ncarry reasoning and tool calls.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
